First Author: Miller
Title: Predictive Abilities of Machine Learning Techniques May Be Limited by Dataset Characteristics: Insights From the UNOS Database
Source number: 38  ￼

Very short summary: This UNOS registry study compared multiple machine-learning and traditional statistical models for predicting 1-year survival after heart transplantation. Five key findings were: (1) 50,453 adult first single-organ heart transplant recipients were analyzed after excluding patients with >20% missing data; (2) the primary outcome was 1-year survival; (3) advanced models did not meaningfully outperform traditional models, with all C-statistics remaining modest at about 0.66 or less; (4) the neural network achieved the highest discrimination, C-statistic 0.66, only slightly above logistic regression, ridge regression, and LASSO at 0.65; and (5) model performance was similarly modest across UNOS regions and across 3 major allocation eras, suggesting that dataset characteristics may limit predictive gains from more complex methods. Table 1 and Figures 1–2 were used directly for this extraction.  ￼

